1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
In 2022, a crypto exchange called
FTX collapsed in 72 hours.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,000
Eight billion dollars in
customer funds — gone.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,200
No warning.
No recourse. No refunds.

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,800
That same week, the Bitcoin blockchain
processed over 200,000 transactions.

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,500
Zero errors.
Zero downtime.

6
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,800
Same industry.
Completely different result.

7
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,200
Today, we are going to understand
exactly why.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Every financial system has
the same design flaw.

9
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,500
It requires you to
trust someone else.

10
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,000
When you send money,
you trust your bank.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
You trust their systems.
Their employees. Their decisions.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,500
You trust that they won't fail,
freeze your account, or disappear overnight.

13
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:36,500
For most of history,
that was the only option.

14
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,500
No trusted middleman —
no transaction.

15
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,500
Computer scientists called this
the Byzantine Generals Problem.

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,000
Here's the analogy
that makes it click.

17
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,000
Imagine four generals
surrounding an enemy city.

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
They must agree on a plan:
attack at dawn, or retreat.

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,500
They can only communicate
by messenger.

20
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:00,000
But one general is a traitor.

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The traitor sends different messages
to different generals.

22
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,500
How do you reach agreement —
when you can't trust the messenger?

23
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,500
This problem stumped researchers
for decades.

24
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,500
Then in 2008, an anonymous developer
named Satoshi Nakamoto published a paper.

25
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:20,500
The title: "Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer
Electronic Cash System."

26
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,500
The solution:
a chain of blocks.

27
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Start with the
building block.

28
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
A block is a
container of data.

29
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,500
Think of it like one page
in a shared ledger.

30
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,500
Each page records a
batch of transactions.

31
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:41,500
On Bitcoin, each block holds roughly
2,000 to 3,000 transactions.

32
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:44,500
Once the page fills up —
it's sealed.

33
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,500
No changes.
A new page begins.

34
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
But a blockchain page has three parts
a normal ledger doesn't.

35
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,000
First: the data.

36
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Who sent what to whom,
and how much.

37
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,500
Every transaction in this block,
recorded and verified.

38
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,500
Second: the timestamp.

39
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,500
The exact moment this block
was created and sealed.

40
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,000
Precise, permanent,
and cannot be altered.

41
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,500
Third: the hash.

42
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,000
This is the most critical part
of the entire system.

43
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
We need to understand hashes properly.

44
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,500
Everything else depends on this.

45
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
A hash is a fingerprint
for data.

46
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,500
You feed any input into
a hash function.

47
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,000
It produces a fixed-length
string of characters.

48
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Always the same length.
Always looks random.

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Here is the SHA-256 hash
of the word "Hello":

50
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,500
185f8db32921bd46d35c
c96953e114e6c68af82b...

51
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Here is the hash of "Hello!" —
one character different:

52
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,500
334d016f755cd6dc58c5
3a86e183882f8ec14f52...

53
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:01,500
Completely different output.
From a single character change.

54
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
This is the key property
of a hash.

55
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
You cannot predict the output
without running the function.

56
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
You cannot reverse-engineer
the input from the output.

57
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Change one byte of the input —
the entire output changes.

58
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:20,000
Every block gets a hash.

59
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
It is the fingerprint of
everything inside.

60
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Now here is the chain part.

61
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Every block contains the hash
of the block before it.

62
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
That is the entire chain structure.
Let that sink in.

63
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,000
Block 101 contains the
hash of block 100.

64
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,500
Block 100 contains the
hash of block 99.

65
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:47,000
All the way back to block zero —
called the Genesis Block.

66
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Think of a document
audit trail.

67
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,500
At the top of every new page,
you write a summary of the previous page.

68
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:59,500
If anyone secretly changes a past page —
the summary no longer matches.

69
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,500
The tampering is visible
to anyone who checks.

70
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,500
Now watch what happens when someone
tries to alter the blockchain.

71
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,500
Suppose an attacker wants to change
a transaction from six months ago.

72
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,000
They alter block 500.

73
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:18,000
The moment they change block 500's data —
its hash changes.

74
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Block 501 stored the old hash
of block 500. Now block 501 is invalid.

75
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,500
To fix block 501,
they must recalculate it.

76
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,500
But recalculating block 501
changes its hash.

77
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:32,500
Now block 502 is invalid.
And block 503. All the way to the present.

78
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
In a chain with hundreds of thousands
of blocks — this is computationally enormous.

79
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,500
The past is locked.
This property is called immutability.

80
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,500
Now the final layer.

81
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,000
Who holds this chain?

82
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,500
In a blockchain network, thousands of
computers — called nodes — hold a complete copy.

83
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,500
No central server.
No headquarters.

84
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,000
No single point of failure.

85
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,500
When a new block is proposed, every node
validates it independently.

86
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:05,500
They check: are all
transactions valid?

87
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,500
Does the hash match
the block's contents?

88
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:12,500
Does it correctly reference
the previous block's hash?

89
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,500
Only if the majority agrees —
the block is added to every copy.

90
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Think of it as a town meeting
without a mayor.

91
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
A new rule is proposed.
Everyone votes independently.

92
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
No single person
controls the outcome.

93
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,000
This is called consensus.

94
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,500
Bitcoin uses a mechanism
called Proof of Work.

95
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:38,500
Miners — specialised nodes — compete
to solve a computational puzzle.

96
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:42,000
The first to solve it earns the right
to add the next block.

97
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,500
This puzzle requires real energy.
That energy cost is what makes it expensive to attack.

98
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:50,500
To rewrite Bitcoin's history, an attacker
would need more power than all honest miners.

99
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,500
Today, that would cost
billions of dollars per hour.

100
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
That is why Bitcoin's ledger has never
been successfully altered.

101
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Here is what you
now understand.

102
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
A block is a sealed ledger page —
data, timestamp, and hash.

103
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
A hash is a digital fingerprint.
One byte changed — entirely different.

104
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Each block stores the previous
block's hash. That is the chain.

105
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Thousands of nodes
each hold the full chain.

106
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,500
Altering any past block breaks the chain
forward — every node knows immediately.

107
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
This is why Bitcoin's ledger is 14 years old
and has never been rewritten.

108
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Not because it's protected
by a company.

109
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,500
Because the math makes it
practically impossible.

110
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,500
In the next video, we go deeper.

111
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:41,000
Bitcoin versus Ethereum.

112
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,500
What the 21-million-coin limit
actually means.

113
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:49,000
And why Ethereum introduced programmability
that Bitcoin was never designed for.

